              "text": "At birth, the biological sex ratio of humans is roughly equal, with a slight natural tilt toward males (about 105 boys to 100 girls). Because of this 1:1 ratio, a community where men have multiple wives (polygyny) should theoretically run out of women, leaving a massive surplus of unmarried men. \n\nHowever, in societies and communities where polygyny is common, demographers, anthropologists, and sociologists have identified several mechanisms that balance the marriage market and allow the practice to persist. \n\n---\n\n### 1. The Spousal Age Gap + High Population Growth (The Mathematical Solution)\nThe most significant demographic driver of polygyny is the combination of a **large age gap** between husbands and wives and a **rapidly growing population**. \n\nIn a growing population, each successive generation (or \"birth cohort\") is larger than the one before it. If men marry women who are significantly younger than themselves, they are drawing from a younger, larger cohort. \n* **The Math:** If a community's population grows at 3% per year and there is a 10-year age gap between husbands and wives (e.g., men marry at 28, women at 18), the cohort of 18-year-old women will be roughly **34% larger** than the cohort of 28-year-old men. \n* This natural \"cohort surplus\" of younger women allows men to have, on average, more than one wife without mathematically shutting other men out of the marriage market.\n\n### 2. Delayed Marriage for Men\nIn polygynous societies, women usually marry very young (often right at the onset of adulthood), while men must wait much longer. \nBefore a man can marry, he typically must accumulate wealth, build a home, or secure enough resources to pay a \"bride price\" to the woman's family. This means that at any given time, a large portion of the young male population is temporarily \"unmarriageable\" or waiting in line, while virtually 100% of the adult female population is married. This delays the entry of men into the marriage pool, temporarily shifting the active ratio of available partners in favor of older men.\n\n### 3. Highly Unequal Distribution (Most Men are Monogamous)\nIt is a common misconception that *all* men in polygamous societies have multiple wives. In reality, polygyny is highly stratified by wealth, power, and age. \n* The majority of men in these societies actually have only one wife, or struggle to marry at all. \n* Only a wealthy, older, or high-status elite (such as chiefs, elders, or wealthy landowners) can afford to support multiple wives and pay multiple bride prices. \nBecause the \"average\" number of wives per man is dragged down by the many monogamous or unmarried men, the overall demand for women is kept within mathematically possible limits.\n\n### 4. Active Exclusion: The \"Lost Boys\" Phenomenon\nIn tight-knit, fundamentalist, or closed polygamous sects (such as the Fundamentalist Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-Day Saints, or FLDS, in North America), the math is forced to work through social expulsion. \nBecause older, powerful men in these sects command multiple wives, there is a severe shortage of young women for the teenage boys. To resolve this, church leadership often systematically excommunicates, kicks out, or pressures young teenage boys to leave the community over minor infractions (such as listening to music, wearing certain clothes, or showing interest in girls). These young men are known colloquially as the **\"Lost Boys.\"** By forcing young males out, the community artificially skews the male-to-female ratio, leaving more young women for the remaining older men.\n\n### 5. Historical Factors: High Male Mortality\nHistorically, the math of polygyny was often balanced by high male mortality. In traditional or historical societies, men faced much higher mortality rates than women due to:\n* Warfare and inter-tribal conflicts\n* Dangerous hunting, fishing, or physical labor\n* Travel and exploration \nThis created a genuine surplus of adult women and widows. In these contexts, polygyny functioned as a social safety net, ensuring that widows and fatherless children were absorbed into existing households and economically supported.\n\n### 6. Selective Migration and Outsourcing\nIn some communities, the gender ratio is maintained by selectively bringing women in from the outside while encouraging men to leave. \n* **In-migration:** Polygamous communities may actively recruit or convert young women from other regions or mainstream society. \n* **Out-migration:** Young men may be encouraged to leave the community for outside work, higher education, or military service, effectively reducing the number of local males competing for wives.",
